3
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:07,740
disorders. I'm
Dr. Carl Abraham,

4
00:00:07,740 --> 00:00:09,420
and today we're
going to explore how

5
00:00:09,420 --> 00:00:12,040
sleep works, why it
matters, and what

6
00:00:12,040 --> 00:00:14,400
happens when it
goes wrong. Think of

7
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:16,200
this as your foundation
for understanding

8
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:17,840
one of the most
essential yet

9
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:20,660
often overlooked
physiologic processes.

10
00:00:20,790 --> 00:00:23,700
I don't know about
any of you. It's also

11
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:25,780
a subject that's very
dear to my heart.

12
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:32,940
We're going to cover
the basics of sleep

13
00:00:32,940 --> 00:00:36,080
physiology, then how
to recognize and manage

14
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:38,400
common sleep disorders,
including when

15
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:41,100
to order a polysomnogram
and when to

16
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:44,160
involve a specialist.
And then we'll finish

17
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:46,740
by talking about sleep
wellness and why it's

18
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:49,060
critical for both
patient safety and

19
00:00:49,060 --> 00:00:51,280
your own performance
as a future physician.

20
00:00:51,660 --> 00:00:54,140
I've included some
connections to other

21
00:00:54,140 --> 00:00:56,360
lectures on this
slide, as well as

22
00:00:56,360 --> 00:00:58,600
QR codes that pointed
to the scholar

23
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:01,360
Rx Bricks for,
on the top, sleep

24
00:01:01,360 --> 00:01:04,980
physiology and underneath
sleep disorders.

25
00:01:15,830 --> 00:01:18,190
We're going to start
with sleep physiology

26
00:01:18,190 --> 00:01:21,510
in order to understand
what is healthy

27
00:01:21,510 --> 00:01:24,770
before we take a look
at what is a disorder.

28
00:01:28,390 --> 00:01:31,390
So, as you know, we
assess sleep health in

29
00:01:31,390 --> 00:01:34,050
every patient as part
of the social history.

30
00:01:34,270 --> 00:01:37,110
Sleep needs change
dramatically

31
00:01:37,110 --> 00:01:38,470
across the lifespan.

32
00:01:39,070 --> 00:01:42,190
Newborns may sleep
up to 20 hours a day.

33
00:01:42,270 --> 00:01:44,330
Children and
adolescents still

34
00:01:44,330 --> 00:01:46,510
require much more
sleep than adults.

35
00:01:46,510 --> 00:01:48,990
Most adults need
somewhere between

36
00:01:48,990 --> 00:01:50,890
seven to nine
hours every night.

37
00:01:50,890 --> 00:01:54,050
There are cultural and
regional differences,

38
00:01:54,050 --> 00:01:56,490
for example,
siestas, that still

39
00:01:56,490 --> 00:02:00,770
result in normal
amounts of sleep. and as

40
00:02:00,770 --> 00:02:03,250
we age, especially
as we become elderly,

41
00:02:03,450 --> 00:02:05,450
we tend to go to
sleep earlier.

42
00:02:07,950 --> 00:02:10,430
Our sleep-wake
cycle is governed by

43
00:02:10,430 --> 00:02:13,130
circlinging rhythms.
These are internal

44
00:02:13,130 --> 00:02:15,470
biological clocks present
in almost all living

45
00:02:15,470 --> 00:02:18,050
organisms. Without
sunlight or other

46
00:02:18,050 --> 00:02:20,890
external clues, the
human natural rhythm

47
00:02:20,890 --> 00:02:24,330
actually runs closer
to 25 hours. These

48
00:02:24,330 --> 00:02:26,150
rhythms shape our
physical, mental, and

49
00:02:26,150 --> 00:02:28,150
behavioral patterns
throughout the day.

50
00:02:28,270 --> 00:02:31,930
If it isn't obvious,
sleep is a repeating

51
00:02:31,930 --> 00:02:34,590
cyclical behavior
that follows the

52
00:02:34,590 --> 00:02:37,950
circadian or 24-hour
rhythm. The processes

53
00:02:37,950 --> 00:02:40,670
that maintain homeostasis
differ between

54
00:02:40,670 --> 00:02:43,290
wakefulness and
sleep. For example,

55
00:02:43,710 --> 00:02:45,890
wakefulness is marked
by feeding while

56
00:02:45,890 --> 00:02:48,990
sleeping is a fasting
state. As you can

57
00:02:48,990 --> 00:02:51,430
see on this slide,
many metabolic and

58
00:02:51,430 --> 00:02:53,610
cannabolic processes
are influenced by these

59
00:02:53,610 --> 00:02:56,810
states. For example,
muscle glycolysis

60
00:02:56,810 --> 00:02:59,590
and cortisol, bile
acid, and insulin

61
00:02:59,590 --> 00:03:02,130
secretion occur more
during the waking state,

62
00:03:02,130 --> 00:03:05,150
while melatonin,
growth hormone, and

63
00:03:05,150 --> 00:03:08,230
leptin secretion, and
oxidative metabolism

64
00:03:08,230 --> 00:03:11,170
in muscle primarily
occurs during sleep.

65
00:03:11,970 --> 00:03:14,850
Importantly, animal
models have shown

66
00:03:14,850 --> 00:03:17,890
that CNS neurotoxins
such as amyloid,

67
00:03:17,890 --> 00:03:20,090
beta, and tau
are cleared more

68
00:03:20,090 --> 00:03:23,010
efficiently during
sleep. In addition,

69
00:03:23,330 --> 00:03:25,710
although they are
poorly understood,

70
00:03:26,050 --> 00:03:28,310
dreams may function
to reduce stress

71
00:03:28,310 --> 00:03:30,610
and sleep is important
in consolidating

72
00:03:30,610 --> 00:03:32,590
memory, a fact
you can use to

73
00:03:32,590 --> 00:03:34,510
improve the efficiency
of studying.

74
00:03:34,510 --> 00:03:37,390
So the bottom
line, sleep is far

75
00:03:37,390 --> 00:03:39,790
more than just
rest for the body.

76
00:03:41,730 --> 00:03:44,050
And key tool
in evaluating

77
00:03:44,050 --> 00:03:46,030
sleep disorders is
the polysomnogram

78
00:03:46,030 --> 00:03:48,290
or overnight sleep study.

79
00:03:48,410 --> 00:03:51,030
It records brain
waves, eye movements,

80
00:03:51,030 --> 00:03:53,150
muscle tone, heart
rhythm, breathing

81
00:03:53,150 --> 00:03:55,730
patterns, oxygen levels,
and limb movements.

82
00:03:56,650 --> 00:03:59,230
This comprehensive
monitoring makes it

83
00:03:59,230 --> 00:04:01,730
the gold standard for
diagnosing conditions

84
00:04:01,730 --> 00:04:04,310
such as sleep
apnea, narcolepsy,

85
00:04:04,310 --> 00:04:06,950
parasomnias, and the
rest of Flegge syndrome,

86
00:04:06,950 --> 00:04:08,910
all of which we'll
talk about later.

87
00:04:09,030 --> 00:04:11,850
The picture on the
top right shows lead

88
00:04:11,850 --> 00:04:14,170
placement for an
electroencephalogram.

89
00:04:14,290 --> 00:04:16,810
These leads then
feed into a computer,

90
00:04:16,810 --> 00:04:19,090
previously onto
a piece of paper,

91
00:04:19,090 --> 00:04:20,870
sort of like a
lie detector test,

92
00:04:20,870 --> 00:04:24,130
that transcribed the
electrical activity

93
00:04:24,130 --> 00:04:26,070
in various places
in the brain.

94
00:04:26,110 --> 00:04:28,850
The result are the
patterns to the right,

95
00:04:28,850 --> 00:04:30,690
which show the
different types of waves

96
00:04:30,690 --> 00:04:32,950
seen in the different
stages of sleep.

97
00:04:32,950 --> 00:04:36,210
You can also see that
as we fall into deeper

98
00:04:36,210 --> 00:04:38,610
and deeper sleep,
the cycles of brain

99
00:04:38,610 --> 00:04:42,130
electrical activity slow.
beta waves are rapid

100
00:04:42,130 --> 00:04:44,270
frequency waves that
are present while awake

101
00:04:44,270 --> 00:04:47,670
and active alpha waves
are present while awake

102
00:04:47,670 --> 00:04:51,170
and at rest theta
waves are present at

103
00:04:51,170 --> 00:04:54,030
early stages of sleep
and delta waves the

104
00:04:54,030 --> 00:04:58,090
slowest waves during deep
sleep not shown is the

105
00:04:58,090 --> 00:05:01,310
brain activity during
REM or REM which is

106
00:05:01,310 --> 00:05:04,070
erratic alternating
between slow and fast

107
00:05:04,070 --> 00:05:06,890
frequency waves the
picture on the bottom is

108
00:05:06,890 --> 00:05:09,750
an example of one person
who underwent a sleep

109
00:05:09,750 --> 00:05:12,470
study. One of the
misunderstandings of the

110
00:05:12,470 --> 00:05:14,550
stages of sleep is
that we cycle through

111
00:05:14,550 --> 00:05:19,030
the stages in order and
then enter REM and then

112
00:05:19,030 --> 00:05:21,730
move back through
the stages in reverse

113
00:05:21,730 --> 00:05:24,470
order. That's actually
not true. As you can

114
00:05:24,470 --> 00:05:27,790
see, this particular
patient enters REM sleep

115
00:05:27,790 --> 00:05:30,030
later in the night
without entering the

116
00:05:30,030 --> 00:05:33,910
deepest stages of sleep,
which is typical. The

117
00:05:33,910 --> 00:05:35,890
time the patient goes
to bed until the patient

118
00:05:35,890 --> 00:05:38,680
falls asleep is called
the sleep latency.

119
00:05:38,750 --> 00:05:42,150
In this patient, it's
about 30 minutes. The

120
00:05:42,150 --> 00:05:44,450
time from the beginning
of sleep until the

121
00:05:44,450 --> 00:05:48,590
first REM sleep is
called the REM latency.

122
00:05:51,030 --> 00:05:53,310
Sleep architecture
refers to the

123
00:05:53,310 --> 00:05:55,470
structure of sleep
across the night.

124
00:05:55,470 --> 00:05:57,930
The American Academy
of Sleep defines

125
00:05:57,930 --> 00:06:00,490
wakefulness and
the stages of sleep

126
00:06:00,490 --> 00:06:04,490
N1, N2, N3, and
REM based on the

127
00:06:04,490 --> 00:06:06,550
distinct EEG
patterns and physical

128
00:06:06,550 --> 00:06:08,930
characteristics of
each of these states.

129
00:06:09,190 --> 00:06:11,270
Wakefulness, or stage W,

130
00:06:11,390 --> 00:06:14,910
here refers to awake
at breast. Alpha waves

131
00:06:14,910 --> 00:06:17,570
on EEG are 9 to 11
hertz, and there is

132
00:06:17,570 --> 00:06:19,710
very little muscle
tone except for in the

133
00:06:19,710 --> 00:06:24,010
face. As we drift
toward sleep, stage N1,

134
00:06:24,190 --> 00:06:25,710
EEG slows,

135
00:06:26,030 --> 00:06:28,890
eyelids droop, and
pupils constrict,

136
00:06:28,890 --> 00:06:32,150
and the eyes may rove
from side to side.

137
00:06:32,330 --> 00:06:34,930
Stage N2 is
characterized by slower

138
00:06:34,930 --> 00:06:38,710
waves overall, but
also sleek spindles.

139
00:06:38,810 --> 00:06:40,830
These are faster
waves detected

140
00:06:40,830 --> 00:06:42,570
at the bitemporal reads.

141
00:06:42,890 --> 00:06:44,790
Delta waves are
characteristic

142
00:06:44,790 --> 00:06:46,530
of deepest sleep.

143
00:06:46,870 --> 00:06:49,910
REM EEG patterns are
erratic, and there

144
00:06:49,910 --> 00:06:51,970
is a further decrease
in muscle tone

145
00:06:51,970 --> 00:06:54,790
except for the
extraocular muscles,

146
00:06:54,790 --> 00:06:57,990
resulting in bursts of
rapid eye movements.

147
00:07:00,830 --> 00:07:02,030
Physiologically,

148
00:07:02,510 --> 00:07:05,950
non-REM and REM sleep
differ in important

149
00:07:05,950 --> 00:07:10,190
ways. Non-REM is
characterized by stable vital

150
00:07:10,190 --> 00:07:12,930
signs and increased
parasympathetic tone.

151
00:07:14,050 --> 00:07:17,390
Dreams occur in non
-REM sleep, but they are

152
00:07:17,390 --> 00:07:20,470
much less vivid and
intense compared to

153
00:07:20,470 --> 00:07:23,010
the dreaming that
characterizes REM sleep.

154
00:07:23,510 --> 00:07:27,030
REM brings bursts of
autonomic activity,

155
00:07:27,070 --> 00:07:29,490
irregular heart
rhythm, decreased

156
00:07:29,490 --> 00:07:31,550
thermoregulation,
vivid dreaming,

157
00:07:31,550 --> 00:07:34,470
and near-complete
muscle paralysis,

158
00:07:34,470 --> 00:07:36,870
except for the
extraocular movements.

159
00:07:37,890 --> 00:07:40,550
Now that we've covered
normal sleep, let's

160
00:07:40,550 --> 00:07:42,830
look at sleep disorders,
conditions that

161
00:07:42,830 --> 00:07:46,210
disrupt sleep quality,
timing, or physiology.

162
00:07:48,010 --> 00:07:52,690
has fallen to two broad
categories, disomnias

163
00:07:52,690 --> 00:07:55,730
and parasomnias.
Disomnias are disorders

164
00:07:55,730 --> 00:07:59,030
of sleep-wake time
and or quality, while

165
00:07:59,030 --> 00:08:02,350
parasomnias are physical
behaviors such as

166
00:08:02,350 --> 00:08:06,150
sleepwalking or experiences
like sleep terrors that

167
00:08:06,150 --> 00:08:08,830
occur during or around
the time of sleep.

168
00:08:09,270 --> 00:08:11,710
Disomnias can also
be broken down

169
00:08:11,710 --> 00:08:14,170
into two categories,
primary or

170
00:08:14,170 --> 00:08:17,970
extrinsic, and
secondary or extrinsic.

171
00:08:18,070 --> 00:08:21,150
Primary dysomnias
include primary

172
00:08:21,150 --> 00:08:23,090
insomnia, which
we'll talk about in a

173
00:08:23,090 --> 00:08:25,690
minute, sleep-related
movement disorders,

174
00:08:25,690 --> 00:08:28,150
such as restless
leg syndrome, or

175
00:08:28,150 --> 00:08:30,290
central disorders
of hypersomnolence,

176
00:08:30,290 --> 00:08:31,910
for example, narcolepsy.

177
00:08:32,210 --> 00:08:35,710
Secondary or extrinsic
dysomnias include

178
00:08:35,710 --> 00:08:38,610
secondary insomnia,
in other words, the

179
00:08:38,610 --> 00:08:41,310
insomnia that's caused
by mostly psychiatric

180
00:08:41,310 --> 00:08:45,150
disorders or other
things, but again we'll

181
00:08:45,150 --> 00:08:48,050
talk about that again
in the next few slides,

182
00:08:48,150 --> 00:08:51,610
circadian sleep
rhythm disorders, for

183
00:08:51,610 --> 00:08:55,130
example jet lag, or
other medical illnesses

184
00:08:55,130 --> 00:08:57,950
that specifically cause
trouble sleeping. A

185
00:08:57,950 --> 00:09:00,430
very common one is
obstructive sleep apnea,

186
00:09:00,430 --> 00:09:03,030
but any other illness
that affects sleep

187
00:09:03,030 --> 00:09:07,070
can be a secondary
cause of insomnia. For

188
00:09:07,070 --> 00:09:09,270
example, congestive
heart failure that

189
00:09:09,270 --> 00:09:12,390
causes frequent paroxysmal
nocturnal dyspnea,

190
00:09:12,850 --> 00:09:16,110
nocturnal reflux
esophagitis, or even

191
00:09:16,110 --> 00:09:19,530
acute illnesses such
as an infection.

192
00:09:22,450 --> 00:09:26,170
It occurs episodically
in about 95% of adults

193
00:09:26,170 --> 00:09:29,090
at some point, but
it's chronic in only

194
00:09:29,090 --> 00:09:31,970
about 10% adults.
Patients complain of not

195
00:09:31,970 --> 00:09:34,840
being able to fall asleep,
daytime sleepiness,

196
00:09:34,840 --> 00:09:37,710
and or fatigue or not
having enough energy.

197
00:09:37,910 --> 00:09:40,730
Primary insomnia, in
other words, insomnia

198
00:09:40,730 --> 00:09:42,950
for which no secondary
cause is found,

199
00:09:42,950 --> 00:09:46,210
makes up only about
10% of those with

200
00:09:46,210 --> 00:09:49,190
insomnia. One pattern
of primary insomnia

201
00:09:49,190 --> 00:09:51,130
that has been noted
is that which occurs

202
00:09:51,130 --> 00:09:54,050
after a stressful
period has resolved. The

203
00:09:54,050 --> 00:09:56,970
good news for these
cases is that most

204
00:09:56,970 --> 00:09:59,470
of the time this
resolves spontaneously.

205
00:10:00,930 --> 00:10:04,370
About 90% of those
with insomnia have

206
00:10:04,370 --> 00:10:07,510
secondary insomnia.
About 50% of these

207
00:10:07,510 --> 00:10:09,810
cases are due to a
psychiatric condition

208
00:10:09,810 --> 00:10:13,910
such as depression,
anxiety, mania, PTSD,

209
00:10:14,050 --> 00:10:15,950
ADHD, or OCD.

210
00:10:16,410 --> 00:10:19,290
Medications that may
cause secondary insomnia

211
00:10:19,290 --> 00:10:21,210
include stimulants,
for example,

212
00:10:21,210 --> 00:10:24,270
caffeine, nicotine,
amphetamines, and *******,

213
00:10:24,610 --> 00:10:27,130
depressants such as
opiates, alcohol,

214
00:10:27,130 --> 00:10:29,330
barbiturates, and
benzodiazepines,

215
00:10:29,730 --> 00:10:32,050
hallucinogens like
phancyclidine,

216
00:10:32,090 --> 00:10:36,570
***, *********, and
MDMA, or diuretics that

217
00:10:36,570 --> 00:10:38,370
are prescribed to
the patient close to

218
00:10:38,370 --> 00:10:41,250
bedtime so that they are
going to the bathroom

219
00:10:41,250 --> 00:10:44,310
repeatedly at night,
acute and chronic

220
00:10:44,310 --> 00:10:47,030
medical conditions
such as COPD, systolic

221
00:10:47,030 --> 00:10:50,490
heart failure, BPH, acute
gastroenterocolitis,

222
00:10:50,770 --> 00:10:53,890
or even post-surgical
pain can stop

223
00:10:53,890 --> 00:10:56,310
people from having a
good night's sleep.

224
00:10:58,290 --> 00:11:00,790
Treatment of insomnia
includes treating

225
00:11:00,790 --> 00:11:03,490
underlying conditions or
discontinuing substances

226
00:11:03,490 --> 00:11:05,690
or medications that
promote insomnia

227
00:11:05,690 --> 00:11:09,350
for those with secondary
insomnia, regular

228
00:11:09,350 --> 00:11:13,450
exercise and a nutritious
diet, sleep hygiene,

229
00:11:13,450 --> 00:11:15,270
which we'll talk
about in a minute, and

230
00:11:15,270 --> 00:11:20,170
non-benzodiazepine
hypnotics, such as Zolpidem,

231
00:11:20,250 --> 00:11:23,630
Xaloplon, and Esopoclon.
These medications

232
00:11:23,630 --> 00:11:26,370
can be remembered
by the mnemonic ZZZ.

233
00:11:28,110 --> 00:11:31,250
For insomnia, should
be sleep hygiene,

234
00:11:31,250 --> 00:11:33,350
which we'll talk about
on the next slide.

235
00:11:33,790 --> 00:11:35,710
Pharmacologic
treatment should start

236
00:11:35,710 --> 00:11:39,150
with the ZZZ, non
-benzodiazepine

237
00:11:39,150 --> 00:11:41,490
hypnotics, or
melatonin receptor

238
00:11:41,490 --> 00:11:44,930
agonists. If these
don't work, then

239
00:11:44,950 --> 00:11:47,550
benzodiazepines can
be tried. However,

240
00:11:47,550 --> 00:11:50,610
there are disadvantages
to benzodiazepines.

241
00:11:50,610 --> 00:11:52,890
They disrupt the
sleep cycle and

242
00:11:52,890 --> 00:11:56,050
specifically prevent
deep sleep. They may be

243
00:11:56,050 --> 00:11:57,990
addictive and they
are associated with

244
00:11:57,990 --> 00:12:00,510
falls that lead to
trauma in the elderly.

245
00:12:00,510 --> 00:12:03,590
If benzodiazepines
don't work, then

246
00:12:03,590 --> 00:12:07,230
antipsychotics, antidepressants,
and antihistamines

247
00:12:07,230 --> 00:12:09,790
can be tried. These
are last resorts

248
00:12:09,790 --> 00:12:12,470
due to very high
side effect profiles.

249
00:12:13,350 --> 00:12:15,670
If the treatment
scheme is different

250
00:12:15,670 --> 00:12:18,170
than this for specific
sleep disorders,

251
00:12:18,170 --> 00:12:20,470
I'll mention it when
we talk about them.

252
00:12:22,930 --> 00:12:25,470
The attempted prior
to pharmacologic

253
00:12:25,470 --> 00:12:27,670
therapy and
pharmacologic therapy

254
00:12:27,670 --> 00:12:29,510
should not be given
without attention

255
00:12:29,510 --> 00:12:31,610
to sleep hygiene
because of its influence

256
00:12:31,610 --> 00:12:34,370
on sleep. What
is sleep hygiene?

257
00:12:34,610 --> 00:12:36,210
The patient should
wake up in the

258
00:12:36,210 --> 00:12:37,790
morning and go to
sleep at night about

259
00:12:37,790 --> 00:12:40,390
the same time every
day. They should

260
00:12:40,390 --> 00:12:43,410
avoid late-day
caffeine, alcohol, large

261
00:12:43,410 --> 00:12:45,930
meals, and screens
close to bedtime.

262
00:12:46,170 --> 00:12:49,790
And sleep should happen
in a quiet, dark,

263
00:12:49,790 --> 00:12:53,010
stress, phone, and
pet-free environment.

264
00:12:53,090 --> 00:12:55,570
Many of us don't
follow this regimen,

265
00:12:55,570 --> 00:12:57,990
but research has
shown that this is how

266
00:12:57,990 --> 00:13:00,490
to get a good night's
sleep most nights.

267
00:13:01,610 --> 00:13:04,450
Let's start talking
about specific sleep

268
00:13:04,450 --> 00:13:07,390
disorders Although
restless leg syndrome is

269
00:13:07,390 --> 00:13:09,950
considered a sleep
movement disorder The

270
00:13:09,950 --> 00:13:12,670
patient's symptoms occur
while awake and actually

271
00:13:12,670 --> 00:13:15,790
prevent sleep The
patient experiences leg

272
00:13:15,790 --> 00:13:18,150
burning, itching,
or tingling that is

273
00:13:18,150 --> 00:13:22,350
relieved by movement It
occurs in about 5-15% of

274
00:13:22,350 --> 00:13:24,470
the population at
some time during their

275
00:13:24,470 --> 00:13:27,910
life The cause is
unknown in most cases but

276
00:13:27,910 --> 00:13:30,730
in a few, it may be
secondary to pregnancy,

277
00:13:30,850 --> 00:13:31,890
iron deficiency,

278
00:13:32,110 --> 00:13:33,530
peripheral neuropathy,

279
00:13:33,610 --> 00:13:35,930
chronic kidney
disease, or ADHD.

280
00:13:36,790 --> 00:13:37,910
Importantly,

281
00:13:38,250 --> 00:13:41,350
restless leg syndrome
is not the presenting

282
00:13:41,350 --> 00:13:43,710
symptom in any of
these except for iron

283
00:13:43,710 --> 00:13:47,090
deficiency. So any
patient with restless leg

284
00:13:47,090 --> 00:13:49,890
syndrome should have
iron studies and iron

285
00:13:49,890 --> 00:13:52,350
deficiency should be
treated if present.

286
00:13:52,850 --> 00:13:54,950
Specific treatment
for restless

287
00:13:54,950 --> 00:13:56,730
leg syndrome
is pregabalin,

288
00:13:56,730 --> 00:13:59,110
dopamine agonists,
and gampapentin.

289
00:13:59,170 --> 00:14:01,690
The restless leg
syndrome that

290
00:14:01,690 --> 00:14:03,110
is associated
with pregnancy

291
00:14:03,110 --> 00:14:05,590
usually resolves
after delivery.

292
00:14:06,510 --> 00:14:09,870
The circadian rhythm
sleep-wake disorders

293
00:14:09,870 --> 00:14:12,910
are those which
disrupt sleep timing.

294
00:14:12,910 --> 00:14:15,530
For example, the most
common is probably

295
00:14:15,530 --> 00:14:17,870
jet lag, the
treatment of which is

296
00:14:17,870 --> 00:14:20,730
usually melatonin,
although a recent study

297
00:14:20,730 --> 00:14:23,190
suggested that if one
can stay awake while

298
00:14:23,190 --> 00:14:25,320
the sun is up at
your destination and

299
00:14:25,320 --> 00:14:27,370
wait to go to sleep
when the sun goes

300
00:14:27,370 --> 00:14:30,230
down, the severity of
jet lag is reduced.

301
00:14:31,030 --> 00:14:33,430
Uncommon causes
of circadian

302
00:14:33,430 --> 00:14:36,070
rhythm sleep-wake
disorders include

303
00:14:36,070 --> 00:14:38,570
delayed sleep
phase disorder.

304
00:14:38,610 --> 00:14:40,950
People with this
go to bed and

305
00:14:40,950 --> 00:14:43,630
wake up later than
the usual times.

306
00:14:43,630 --> 00:14:45,970
It may occur in
those who are blind

307
00:14:45,970 --> 00:14:48,690
and those who do
night shift work.

308
00:14:48,910 --> 00:14:51,590
Advanced sleep
phase disorder is

309
00:14:51,590 --> 00:14:53,130
similar, but
instead the patient

310
00:14:53,130 --> 00:14:55,310
goes to bed and
wakes up early.

311
00:14:55,490 --> 00:14:57,570
Treatment for
these include

312
00:14:57,570 --> 00:15:00,290
sleek hygiene,
light therapy, and

313
00:15:00,290 --> 00:15:02,470
melatonin
receptor agonists.

314
00:15:03,750 --> 00:15:06,850
The prototypical
central disorder of

315
00:15:06,850 --> 00:15:10,370
hypersomnolence is narcolepsy.
Think of narcolepsy

316
00:15:10,370 --> 00:15:14,410
as REM intrusion into
wakefulness. There

317
00:15:14,410 --> 00:15:17,570
is sleep paralysis
or the inability to

318
00:15:17,570 --> 00:15:20,930
talk or move on awakening
or falling asleep.

319
00:15:21,670 --> 00:15:24,450
Hypnopopic and hypnagogic

320
00:15:26,900 --> 00:15:29,350
hallucinations are
those that occur when

321
00:15:29,350 --> 00:15:33,230
you wake or fall asleep,
respectively. They

322
00:15:33,230 --> 00:15:35,310
may occur in those
without narcolepsy, but

323
00:15:35,310 --> 00:15:37,950
are much more frequent
in those with it.

324
00:15:38,050 --> 00:15:41,330
Those with narcolepsy
may also have cataplexy

325
00:15:41,330 --> 00:15:44,470
after experiencing
strong emotions that

326
00:15:44,470 --> 00:15:47,890
can result in sometimes
significant trauma.

327
00:15:48,270 --> 00:15:51,050
Patients complain of
daytime sleepiness.

328
00:15:51,290 --> 00:15:53,630
Onset is usually in
young adults and the

329
00:15:53,630 --> 00:15:56,050
condition is not
progressive. It occurs

330
00:15:56,050 --> 00:15:58,750
in less than 0.2% of
the U.S. population

331
00:15:58,750 --> 00:16:02,150
with peaks at 15
and 35 years of age.

332
00:16:02,270 --> 00:16:08,190
90% of those have
an HLA-DQB1-0602

333
00:16:09,090 --> 00:16:11,250
allele, which is
present much less

334
00:16:11,250 --> 00:16:13,430
commonly in those
without narcolepsy.

335
00:16:13,670 --> 00:16:15,670
Studies have shown
that patients may

336
00:16:15,670 --> 00:16:17,830
have decreased
orexin production.

337
00:16:17,970 --> 00:16:21,210
Onset may follow a
viral or bacterial

338
00:16:21,210 --> 00:16:23,850
infection. The
diagnosis is made

339
00:16:23,850 --> 00:16:26,710
by polysomnogram,
which shows rapid

340
00:16:26,710 --> 00:16:29,910
sleep onset,
short REM latency.

341
00:16:30,530 --> 00:16:33,670
Shorter REM latency
is associated

342
00:16:33,670 --> 00:16:35,530
with more severe disease.

343
00:16:36,110 --> 00:16:39,390
Treatment is sleep
hygiene and stimulants,

344
00:16:39,390 --> 00:16:40,750
such as those
was listed on the

345
00:16:40,750 --> 00:16:43,450
slide. Prognosis is
usually excellent

346
00:16:43,450 --> 00:16:45,930
with treatment.
Patients with narcolepsy

347
00:16:45,930 --> 00:16:48,150
commonly require
concomitment

348
00:16:48,150 --> 00:16:51,790
treatment for depression
and or anxiety.

349
00:16:53,750 --> 00:16:56,970
Obstructive sleep apnea
is a sleep-related

350
00:16:56,970 --> 00:16:59,070
breathing disorder
caused by mechanical

351
00:16:59,070 --> 00:17:02,030
obstruction of excess
soft tissue that

352
00:17:02,030 --> 00:17:04,710
results in a rapid
repetitive airway

353
00:17:04,710 --> 00:17:07,350
collapse, in other
words, snoring, and

354
00:17:07,350 --> 00:17:11,390
hypoxygenation due to
short apneic episodes.

355
00:17:11,510 --> 00:17:13,150
Risk factors include

356
00:17:13,150 --> 00:17:15,110
increased age, obesity,

357
00:17:15,410 --> 00:17:18,590
large tonsils
or a large neck,

358
00:17:19,070 --> 00:17:22,170
polycystic ovary
disease, hypothyroidism,

359
00:17:22,170 --> 00:17:23,890
genetic predisposition,

360
00:17:24,070 --> 00:17:25,570
systolic heart failure,

361
00:17:25,730 --> 00:17:27,710
chronic kidney failure,

362
00:17:27,830 --> 00:17:31,650
alcohol use, smoking,
and the male gender.

363
00:17:32,090 --> 00:17:35,290
Prevalence is
about 32% in U.S.

364
00:17:35,290 --> 00:17:37,170
adults greater
than 20 years old.

365
00:17:37,170 --> 00:17:39,690
Most are male. The
storing results

366
00:17:39,690 --> 00:17:41,630
in the inability
to reach deep

367
00:17:41,630 --> 00:17:44,250
sleep and is often
brought to medical

368
00:17:44,250 --> 00:17:45,990
attention by the
sleep partner.

369
00:17:46,310 --> 00:17:49,030
Diagnosis is by
polysomnogram.

370
00:17:49,430 --> 00:17:51,830
Treatment is continuous
positive airway

371
00:17:51,830 --> 00:17:54,910
pressure, which is shown
in the picture at right.

372
00:17:54,910 --> 00:17:59,210
A tightly fitting
mask delivers air at

373
00:17:59,210 --> 00:18:02,730
increased pressure that
keeps the airways open

374
00:18:02,730 --> 00:18:05,690
and prevents the
collapse of soft tissue.

375
00:18:05,710 --> 00:18:09,440
Patients may require a
uvulopoadopharyngeoplasty

376
00:18:09,950 --> 00:18:13,530
that repositions
or resex the uvula,

377
00:18:13,670 --> 00:18:16,110
resex the tussles,
and enlarges the

378
00:18:16,110 --> 00:18:18,350
pharynx, but this
is rarely done.

379
00:18:19,490 --> 00:18:22,610
So let's leave the
disomnias and talk about

380
00:18:22,610 --> 00:18:25,390
parasomnias. Remember
the parasomnias

381
00:18:25,390 --> 00:18:28,050
are behaviors or
experiences that occur

382
00:18:28,050 --> 00:18:31,210
during sleep. We'll talk
about the most common

383
00:18:31,210 --> 00:18:34,650
and there are many
others. The parasomnias

384
00:18:34,650 --> 00:18:36,970
are also categorized
by which stage of

385
00:18:36,970 --> 00:18:39,090
sleep in which they
occur. The first

386
00:18:39,090 --> 00:18:41,630
parasomnia we'll talk
about, sleep terrors,

387
00:18:41,630 --> 00:18:45,510
occurs in stage N3.
About 1% of children

388
00:18:45,510 --> 00:18:47,830
will have them. The
child wakes at night

389
00:18:47,830 --> 00:18:50,930
scared, upset, maybe
yelling, maybe panicking,

390
00:18:50,930 --> 00:18:53,970
and is completely
inconsolable. There

391
00:18:53,970 --> 00:18:56,390
may be associated
autonomic sympathetic

392
00:18:56,390 --> 00:18:59,170
activation with
tachycardia, tachypnea, and

393
00:18:59,170 --> 00:19:01,770
diaphoresis. The
child eventually calms

394
00:19:01,770 --> 00:19:04,310
down, goes back to
sleep, and in the morning

395
00:19:04,310 --> 00:19:06,730
has no recollection
that this occurred.

396
00:19:06,970 --> 00:19:09,310
Prognosis is excellent
with the sleep

397
00:19:09,310 --> 00:19:11,810
terrors eventually
resolving spontaneously.

398
00:19:12,530 --> 00:19:16,170
Another parasomnia,
sleepwalking or somnambulism

399
00:19:16,170 --> 00:19:20,410
also occurs in stage
N3. Despite being

400
00:19:20,410 --> 00:19:22,410
asleep, the person
appears awake and

401
00:19:22,410 --> 00:19:24,990
performs activities
such as walking, working

402
00:19:24,990 --> 00:19:27,390
in the kitchen, or
many other types of

403
00:19:27,390 --> 00:19:30,290
activities and has no
memory of the event the

404
00:19:30,290 --> 00:19:33,070
next morning. This
occurs in up to 30% of

405
00:19:33,070 --> 00:19:35,650
the U.S. population as
a single non-repeating

406
00:19:35,650 --> 00:19:38,570
event, but chronic
sleepwalking may affect

407
00:19:38,570 --> 00:19:42,070
up to 2% of the adult
population. Treatment

408
00:19:42,070 --> 00:19:43,970
is difficult and
centers around keeping

409
00:19:43,970 --> 00:19:46,290
the patient safe,
since some of the

410
00:19:46,290 --> 00:19:48,150
activities such as walking
out of the house or

411
00:19:48,150 --> 00:19:50,790
working in the kitchen
are dangerous. It

412
00:19:50,790 --> 00:19:53,310
usually resolves
spontaneously in children.

413
00:19:53,310 --> 00:19:55,870
If it doesn't in
adults, underlying

414
00:19:55,870 --> 00:19:58,350
medical or psychological
conditions that are

415
00:19:58,350 --> 00:20:00,430
the root of the
behavior should be

416
00:20:00,430 --> 00:20:02,770
treated, and some
patients are treated with

417
00:20:02,770 --> 00:20:05,710
benzodiazepines with
varying success.

418
00:20:07,050 --> 00:20:09,970
The last parasomnia
we'll talk about

419
00:20:09,970 --> 00:20:12,510
is REM sleep
behavior disorder.

420
00:20:12,510 --> 00:20:15,470
This occurs in adults
who yell, kick,

421
00:20:15,470 --> 00:20:19,130
punch, grab, jump, or
perform other aggressive

422
00:20:19,130 --> 00:20:21,350
or violent behaviors
while asleep.

423
00:20:21,350 --> 00:20:24,150
It usually occurs
in older persons

424
00:20:24,150 --> 00:20:26,150
who remember dreams
that explain their

425
00:20:26,150 --> 00:20:28,370
behavior, but they
don't remember

426
00:20:28,370 --> 00:20:30,610
performing the behavior
while sleeping.

427
00:20:31,310 --> 00:20:34,130
Polysomnobram
shows REM sleep

428
00:20:34,130 --> 00:20:36,710
despite the
lack of atonia.

429
00:20:36,800 --> 00:20:39,130
Treatment is
melatonin or low

430
00:20:39,130 --> 00:20:41,890
-dose clonazepam.
Very importantly,

431
00:20:42,270 --> 00:20:45,190
brainstem histology
at autoxy has shown

432
00:20:45,190 --> 00:20:47,750
Lewy bodies that
are also seen in

433
00:20:47,750 --> 00:20:50,910
Parkinson's,
multisystem atrophy, and

434
00:20:50,910 --> 00:20:53,610
Lewy body dementia,
and some feel that

435
00:20:53,610 --> 00:20:56,810
REM sleep behavior
disorder is an early

436
00:20:56,810 --> 00:20:58,950
manifestation of
these conditions.

437
00:20:59,870 --> 00:21:02,490
We've talked about
sleep physiology and

438
00:21:02,490 --> 00:21:04,790
sleep disorders. Now
let's apply these

439
00:21:04,790 --> 00:21:06,470
concepts in a way
that highlights the

440
00:21:06,470 --> 00:21:08,390
importance of sleep
wellness to a subject

441
00:21:08,390 --> 00:21:11,730
that we as students
of medicine should be

442
00:21:11,730 --> 00:21:15,490
at the top of our
minds, patient safety.

443
00:21:17,290 --> 00:21:19,490
Before we talk
about patient safety

444
00:21:19,490 --> 00:21:22,510
specifically, let's clear
up some myths and define

445
00:21:22,510 --> 00:21:24,190
the negative health
outcomes of sleep

446
00:21:24,190 --> 00:21:28,050
deprivation. There is a
very rare prion disease

447
00:21:28,050 --> 00:21:30,930
called fatal familial
insomnia that results

448
00:21:30,930 --> 00:21:33,510
in progressive insomnia
and eventual death

449
00:21:33,510 --> 00:21:36,710
in all patients. This
suggests that the

450
00:21:36,710 --> 00:21:40,110
total lack of sleep
can be deadly. However,

451
00:21:40,250 --> 00:21:43,170
based on anecdotal
evidence of people who

452
00:21:43,170 --> 00:21:45,170
have tried to break the
Guinness Book Record

453
00:21:45,170 --> 00:21:48,230
for longest time awake,
we do know some of the

454
00:21:48,230 --> 00:21:50,830
effects of sleep
deprivation and true

455
00:21:50,830 --> 00:21:54,490
fatalities from sleep
deprivation are exceedingly

456
00:21:54,490 --> 00:21:57,450
rare. A few days
without sleep results in

457
00:21:57,450 --> 00:22:00,550
progressive, slowed, and
altered cognition and

458
00:22:00,550 --> 00:22:02,870
motor control.
This is followed by

459
00:22:02,870 --> 00:22:06,630
hallucinations,
paranoia, memory
loss, and eventually

460
00:22:06,630 --> 00:22:09,610
psychosis in some
persons who are sleep

461
00:22:09,610 --> 00:22:13,450
-deprived for long periods.
The Guinness record

462
00:22:13,450 --> 00:22:16,170
for the longest time
awake is 18 days, 21

463
00:22:16,170 --> 00:22:19,930
hours and 40 minutes.
If I remember correctly,

464
00:22:19,930 --> 00:22:22,330
this particular person
had a few symptoms

465
00:22:22,330 --> 00:22:26,330
of sleep deprivation.
However, there have been

466
00:22:26,330 --> 00:22:28,990
enough people trying
for the record who

467
00:22:28,990 --> 00:22:32,290
have had severe symptoms
after sleep deprivation,

468
00:22:32,290 --> 00:22:35,450
some possibly
permanent, that Guinness

469
00:22:35,450 --> 00:22:38,650
no longer monitors or
reports sleep deprivation

470
00:22:38,650 --> 00:22:41,840
records due to the
negative health effects.

471
00:22:41,840 --> 00:22:44,610
And we all know that
sleep deprivation does

472
00:22:44,610 --> 00:22:46,630
increase the risk of
falling asleep at the

473
00:22:46,630 --> 00:22:49,830
wheel and serious motor
vehicle accidents.

474
00:22:50,810 --> 00:22:54,990
One of the most sleep
-deprived population is

475
00:22:54,990 --> 00:22:58,070
house staff, medical,
surgical, psychiatry,

476
00:22:58,070 --> 00:23:00,610
family practice, and
OB residents who are

477
00:23:00,610 --> 00:23:05,170
sometimes up for 24 or
36 hours more at a time.

478
00:23:05,170 --> 00:23:07,870
They make an excellent
study population.

479
00:23:07,870 --> 00:23:11,650
The first study looked
at house staff who are

480
00:23:11,650 --> 00:23:15,610
on call every fourth
night. 44% reported

481
00:23:15,610 --> 00:23:19,310
falling asleep at a
stoplight, 49% fell asleep

482
00:23:19,310 --> 00:23:22,730
at the wheel, and 23
% while the car was in

483
00:23:22,730 --> 00:23:26,530
motion. 90% of these
events occurred post

484
00:23:26,530 --> 00:23:30,550
-call. The second study
shows being sleep-deprived

485
00:23:30,550 --> 00:23:33,310
increases the risk
of near-misses and

486
00:23:33,310 --> 00:23:36,650
accidents in driving
simulations, and the third

487
00:23:36,650 --> 00:23:39,310
shows subjects
performed worse in every

488
00:23:39,310 --> 00:23:42,870
measure of cognitive
function when tested even

489
00:23:42,870 --> 00:23:45,450
with moderate sleep
deprivation and that

490
00:23:45,450 --> 00:23:47,790
prolonged periods of
sleep deprivation for

491
00:23:47,790 --> 00:23:51,650
example four to five
hours of sleep nightly had

492
00:23:51,650 --> 00:23:55,210
a cumulative effect
resulting in increasing

493
00:23:55,210 --> 00:24:00,610
cognitive impairment
not only that but when

494
00:24:00,610 --> 00:24:03,090
you don't get enough
sleep judgment is impaired

495
00:24:03,090 --> 00:24:06,690
and the result is
poor decisions. Sleep

496
00:24:06,690 --> 00:24:09,830
deprivation results in
increased risk-taking,

497
00:24:09,830 --> 00:24:13,330
likely secondary to
suboptimal or abnormal

498
00:24:13,330 --> 00:24:16,910
functioning of the
prefrontal cortex, nucleus

499
00:24:16,910 --> 00:24:20,410
accumbens, and insula.
It results in more

500
00:24:20,410 --> 00:24:23,550
rigid thinking,
perseveration of ideas, and

501
00:24:23,550 --> 00:24:26,090
difficulty in incorporating
new information.

502
00:24:26,090 --> 00:24:29,730
And here's the worst
news. It's likely that

503
00:24:29,730 --> 00:24:33,030
coffee or other
caffeinated beverages can't

504
00:24:33,030 --> 00:24:35,630
stop one who is sleep
deprived from poor

505
00:24:35,630 --> 00:24:39,110
performance, secondary
to cognitive deficits,

506
00:24:39,110 --> 00:24:40,710
making poor decisions

507
00:24:40,710 --> 00:24:43,110
and taking undue risks.

508
00:24:45,270 --> 00:24:47,170
Have you ever
spent more than 24

509
00:24:47,170 --> 00:24:49,110
hours awake and then
two or three days

510
00:24:49,110 --> 00:24:51,110
later had a cold
or a sore throat?

511
00:24:51,190 --> 00:24:53,190
Well, it turns out
that if we don't

512
00:24:53,190 --> 00:24:55,410
get enough sleep,
we are three times

513
00:24:55,410 --> 00:24:57,930
more likely to get
a cold. At less than

514
00:24:57,930 --> 00:24:59,850
five hours a night
chronically, we

515
00:24:59,850 --> 00:25:02,590
are four times as
likely. In addition,

516
00:25:02,850 --> 00:25:05,270
sleep deprivation
results in decreased

517
00:25:05,270 --> 00:25:07,210
antibody responses
to vaccines

518
00:25:07,210 --> 00:25:09,870
and increases the
risk of pneumonia.

519
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:16,090
The truth is, if you
don't take care of

520
00:25:16,090 --> 00:25:18,870
yourself, you really
can't take care of others.

521
00:25:20,210 --> 00:25:23,890
In summary, normal
sleep physiology is

522
00:25:23,890 --> 00:25:25,830
dictated by circadian
rhythms that

523
00:25:25,830 --> 00:25:28,390
orchestrate hormonal and
physiologic functions

524
00:25:28,390 --> 00:25:30,310
that maintain
normal homeostasis.

525
00:25:30,310 --> 00:25:34,330
stasis. Sleep disorders
are prevalent and

526
00:25:34,330 --> 00:25:37,090
many can be diagnosed
by a primary provider

527
00:25:37,090 --> 00:25:39,470
with sleep studies,
while some require

528
00:25:39,470 --> 00:25:41,830
outside consultation
for management.

529
00:25:42,030 --> 00:25:45,010
Sleep is an integral
part of physician

530
00:25:45,010 --> 00:25:48,670
wellness that optimizes
safety and care of the

531
00:25:48,670 --> 00:25:51,690
patient. And please
give us feedback on this

532
00:25:51,690 --> 00:25:54,970
lecture. We really do
value your opinion.

533
00:26:02,530 --> 00:26:05,290
Thank you. Good
luck and stay well.